WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.430
I've said before that the issue of the minimum
wage is a complicated issue.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:13.940
I've been asked before, would a $15 minimum
wage nationally be good or bad for the economy?

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:19.480
And the truth is that a lot of the answer
depends on where you are measuring, whether

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.380
it's good or bad for the economy.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.980
And I've said this before, um, you know, we
could stick to talking points on this show

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.490
and do black and white stuff.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.400
Like everything is either very good or very
bad.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.789
It's all good or all bad.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:31.789
That's not the reality.

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:32.789
Okay?

00:00:32.789 --> 00:00:38.980
So when we think about a $15 minimum wage
in lower cost of living places where a $15

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:46.320
minimum wage might be doubling the prevailing
minimum wage, there could be negative effects

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.000
in some ways in terms of the destabilizing
effect that that could have on the local job

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.000
market, et cetera.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.760
That's a reality.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:01.039
On the other hand, in high cost of living
places, a $15 minimum wage may not even be

00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:03.249
enough, number one.

00:01:03.249 --> 00:01:08.340
And number two is unlikely to hurt the economy
relative to how much it will help people afford

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:13.500
their day to day lives and inject more money
into the wallets of workers.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.010
So we've got to be thoughtful here.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:20.299
There are some who say raising the minimum
wage has no negative impact period.

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:21.299
That's not true.

00:01:21.299 --> 00:01:22.920
I mean that's just not true in all cases.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.630
Of course, there are people on the other side
who say any increase to the minimum wage causes

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.000
layoffs, it causes business closures.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.610
It will blow up the cost of everything because
businesses will just raise prices in order

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:34.840
to account for that.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.189
But of course, that also isn't true today.

00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:41.310
We have a new case study from New York city.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:46.399
New York city raised the minimum wage to $15
an hour a year ago.

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:51.630
The restaurant industry, which is one of the
ones that is often the focus, uh, of minimum

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:52.920
wage increases.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.880
The restaurant industry is doing really well
in New York city.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.820
Despite this, New York city restaurants have
seen increased revenue and they've also seen

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:04.299
increased employment.

00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:11.840
Now, the way they've done it is that prices
have gone up slightly at New York city restaurants,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.230
detractors of the minimum wage will immediately
say, Whoa, Whoa, Whoa.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:17.600
You raise prices.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.890
Customers will stop going out to eat.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.500
The businesses will fail.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:26.980
They'll have to fire everybody, and the minimum
wage increase will backfire, hasn't happened.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.370
And you know why?

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.780
Prices don't have to go up very much.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:36.569
Imagine that the minimum wage goes up, for
example, 8% uh, and imagine that restaurants

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:40.409
account for that by increasing food prices
by 8%.

00:02:40.409 --> 00:02:45.569
Now more than likely, they wouldn't because
the labor costs are only part of what it costs

00:02:45.569 --> 00:02:46.810
to run a restaurant.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:52.160
So an 8% increase in wages would not even
require an 8% increase in prices.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:03:00.269
But okay, imagine that wages go up 8% food
prices go up 8% a $10 burger becomes a $10

00:03:00.269 --> 00:03:01.810
and 80 cents.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:07.690
Now, I'm not going to write that off as nothing,
but for most people, you're not going to go

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:14.470
out to eat a different number of times because
the burger goes from $10 to $10 and 80 cents.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:15.470
This is the reality.

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:16.660
I don't want to be insensitive.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.210
I don't, I'm not ignoring that.

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:26.010
80 cents on a $10 meal can be meaningful to
many people, but the reality is that for the

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:31.330
most part, you're not going to drastically
change your habits if your burger goes from

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:36.710
$10 to $10 and 80 cents but on the other side
of the ledger, everybody who works at the

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:43.100
restaurant and gets a raise because of that
minimum wage increase now has more money to

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:48.910
go out to other restaurants and to patronize
other businesses where they live.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.730
That is hugely stimulative to the economy
overall.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:57.360
Now, there's no question that if the New York
city economy starts to fail, because it's

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.650
been a strong economy in New York city for
the last year, if the New York city economy

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:05.040
starts to fail, it will be bad for lots of
people.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.340
It'll be bad for restaurants.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:10.989
It'll be bad for their workers, but in the
economic environment in New York city of the

00:04:10.989 --> 00:04:15.790
last year, the minimum wage increase has been
a very, very positive thing.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:21.510
Now, there are also trickle up effects to
having better paid workers other than just

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.980
they have money to spend at local businesses.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:28.840
They are happier, they have a higher standard
of living and thus are providing better service,

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.690
which is good for the businesses.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:34.880
They may be able to afford living closer to
work because they can afford it, or having

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.030
a more reliable car to get to work becomes
economically within reach, which makes them

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:41.480
more reliable workers and helps the business.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:47.140
So it's really important to think about these
changes systemically rather than myopically.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:52.840
This is not to pretend that the minimum wage
can be increased endlessly, but no one is

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:57.831
proposing that this is a very common red herring
on the right where they'll say, listen, if

00:04:57.831 --> 00:05:04.360
$15 an hour is better than 10 why not make
the minimum wage $25 an hour or $100 an hour

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.030
or $200 an hour?

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.200
Then everybody will just have tons of money,
right?

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.900
Liberals will hold on a second.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:14.480
$100 an hour is not an equilibrium wage.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.170
$100 an hour is not a wage.

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:22.090
That is the logical living wage based on the
cost of living of any particular place in

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:23.960
the United States of America.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:27.190
It is a distraction and it's really important
not to fall for it.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:35.150
So my sort of view on the left discussing
minimum wage increases is number one, don't

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:42.220
let them set the talking points that we have
to argue against with stupid stuff like, uh,

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:46.141
make the minimum wage $100 an hour if you
just want people to make more money, that's

00:05:46.141 --> 00:05:49.490
stupid that that's not even worth discussing.

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:54.230
But let's not be the ones that pretend that
there is not another side to the ledger.

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:57.890
A minimum wage increases have effects.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.880
They have trickle up effects and they have
trickled down effects.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.390
We don't have to pretend that there's no risk
to a minimum wage increase to be able to justify

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:11.020
it as the right policy in many cases and in
many instances.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:12.020
That's the approach.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:16.130
I think that the left should have and the
more informed we are a about studies case

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:20.140
studies like the one we see in New York city,
the better we will be able to defend the policies

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:21.760
that we want to see.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:22.760
Let's take a quick break.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:24.060
Make sure to give the David Pakman show

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:30.163
like on Facebook at facebook.com/david pattern
show, very quick break and back with much

00:06:30.163 --> 00:06:30.172
more right afterwards

